THE POSITION AFTER TEN LANDINGS: ALL THREE ARMS MOVED, AND THE C ORACLE
MOVED WITH THEM — READ THE RATIOS, NOT THE ABSOLUTE MILLISECONDS.

date:   2026-09-05 (runs 06:17:56 - 06:30:14 UTC)
host:   mac (Apple M4 Pro, aarch64-apple-darwin, 12 cores). Box quiet: see
        `perf_2026-09-05-arm10-quiet.txt` -- a snapshot before and after every
        arm plus one every 15 s inside it, and the ONE arm that caught the
        sibling lane's A/B binaries was discarded and re-run.
tree:   41e5d8f1ab3adcf270b2991354e2ae5cb1eea7d2 (main@origin). The generated
        `.meta` sidecars say `commit: unknown` because the sweep ran from a
        `jj workspace` whose `git rev-parse HEAD` is empty; the sha above is
        `jj log -r main@origin` and is appended to each sidecar by hand.
tool:   tools/perf_gate.sh, 25 interleaved paired rounds per cell, one untimed
        warmup inside each sample, `env -u RUSTFLAGS` (NO -C target-cpu=native,
        confirmed in the harness source), setup excluded on both sides.
C:      the PREBUILT oracle `Bin/Release/libSvtAv1Enc.a` (2026-08-31), NOT
        rebuilt -- the pos1 workspace symlinks the primary checkout's `Bin`,
        `cbuild-static` and `reference/svt-av1`. Generic build: -O3 -DNDEBUG,
        NATIVE=OFF, runtime RTCD. Only the ~200-line driver
        `tools/perf_c_encode/perf_c_encode` was recompiled here.
data:   benchmarks/perf_2026-09-05-arm10-{still,videokey,inter}.{tsv,raw.tsv,meta}
        benchmarks/perf_2026-09-05-arm10-photo-{still,videokey,inter}.{tsv,raw.tsv,meta}
        benchmarks/perf_2026-09-05-arm10-quiet.txt
prior:  benchmarks/perf_2026-09-03-arm9-*.{tsv,meta} + arm9-POSITION.meta

=================== 1. THE POSITION (gradient qp 40, preset 8) ==============

  preset 8, port/C      64      128     256     512    slope ratio  intercept
  still               0.758    1.287   2.011   2.208     2.29x      port -0.005 ms / C 0.130 ms
  videokey            1.176    1.724   2.320   2.228     2.26x      port -0.414 ms / C -0.044 ms
  inter               1.441    1.899   2.425   2.432     2.46x      port -0.441 ms / C  0.054 ms

**Every cell of all three arms is ident=Y** (byte-identical port .obu == C .obu,
per frame on the inter arm), 25/25 rounds, zero ERR lines.

Fits, `ms = intercept + slope * pixels`:
  still     port  32.1050 ms/MP  intercept -0.005 ms | C 14.0162 ms/MP  intercept  0.130 ms
  videokey  port 134.2442 ms/MP  intercept -0.414 ms | C 59.4884 ms/MP  intercept -0.044 ms
  inter     port 176.5407 ms/MP  intercept -0.441 ms | C 71.6416 ms/MP  intercept  0.054 ms

THE INTERCEPT IS A SEPARATE STORY FROM THE SLOPE, and it is why the 64x64 cell
reads 0.758x on the still arm -- the port is FASTER than C at 64x64 there. C
carries a real positive fixed cost per still frame (0.130 ms) where the port's
fit intercept is ~0; a single "2.29x" hides that entirely. On the two video
arms both intercepts are slightly negative, i.e. the linear fit is not
resolving a fixed cost at these four sizes and the 64x64 cell (1.18x / 1.44x)
is the honest statement of the small-frame position, not the intercept.

=================== 2. WHAT MOVED SINCE arm9 ===============================

arm9 = `perf_2026-09-03-arm9-*`, same box, same grid, same harness, tree
`ceedbf3c`. Between the two runs ten-ish landings went in, including
`0ba430ee` (two-phase txt_search), `600c5177` (MDS1 skip), `2c3aeb8d`
(ind-uv), `8fed0aa1` (DPB clone fix), `2d926217` (compute_stats in C's shape),
five duplicate folds and three IntraBC fixes.

  arm       size   port ms          C ms             ratio
  still      64    0.183 ->  0.173   0.228 ->  0.209   0.791 -> 0.758   -4.2 %
  still     128    0.496 ->  0.421   0.355 ->  0.328   1.402 -> 1.287   -8.2 %
  still     256    2.615 ->  2.162   1.177 ->  1.059   2.214 -> 2.011   -9.2 %
  still     512    9.689 ->  8.401   4.093 ->  3.803   2.376 -> 2.208   -7.1 %
  videokey   64    0.682 ->  0.576   0.519 ->  0.490   1.338 -> 1.176  -12.1 %
  videokey  128    2.027 ->  1.746   1.100 ->  1.001   1.850 -> 1.724   -6.8 %
  videokey  256    8.623 ->  7.858   3.607 ->  3.387   2.400 -> 2.320   -3.3 %
  videokey  512   41.810 -> 34.905  16.367 -> 15.659   2.542 -> 2.228  -12.4 %
  inter      64    1.188 ->  0.969   0.741 ->  0.675   1.592 -> 1.441   -9.5 %
  inter     128    2.966 ->  2.503   1.462 ->  1.317   2.040 -> 1.899   -6.9 %
  inter     256   11.221 -> 10.163   4.515 ->  4.207   2.486 -> 2.425   -2.5 %
  inter     512   54.763 -> 46.066  19.804 -> 18.959   2.767 -> 2.432  -12.1 %

  slope ratio:  still 2.46x -> 2.29x   videokey 2.60x -> 2.26x   inter 2.82x -> 2.46x

THE STANDING CAVEAT STILL APPLIES, VERBATIM FROM arm9: the port side drifts
about 3 % between SESSIONS on this box (arm9 measured the same `76fdb802`
binary at 9.493 ms in one session and 9.797 ms in the next), so a position run
cannot resolve a chunk-sized change. What it CAN resolve is the CUMULATIVE
change since arm9, which is what this run is for. Ten of the twelve cells moved
by more than that 3 % band; `videokey 256` (-3.3 %) and `inter 256` (-2.5 %)
sit inside it and should not be read as results on their own.

=================== 3. C-SIDE DRIFT, AND IT IS NOT SMALL ===================

**C got 4-10 % faster too, on an identical `libSvtAv1Enc.a`.** Per cell:
still -8.3 / -7.6 / -10.0 / -7.1 %, videokey -5.6 / -9.0 / -6.1 / -4.3 %,
inter -8.9 / -9.9 / -6.8 / -4.3 %. C's slopes: still 15.0572 -> 14.0162
(-6.9 %), videokey 62.0431 -> 59.4884 (-4.1 %), inter 74.5633 -> 71.6416
(-3.9 %). The library binary is byte-for-byte the one arm9 measured (dated
2026-08-31, never rebuilt); only the driver was recompiled, and the driver does
no encoding.

The consequence for reading this record: the PORT's absolute -9 to -18 % is
NOT all code. Some of it is whatever made C faster -- a colder or quieter box
this session. **The port/C RATIO is the defensible quantity**, and it is the
one reported above. The port's absolute millisecond drop is an upper bound on
what the ten landings bought, not a measurement of it.

=================== 4. REAL CONTENT: photo_cid, 512x512, p2 and p6 =========

Gradient OVERSTATES the gap; a synthetic-only table misreports the position.
Source: `~/work/zen/codec-corpus` @ 0785871,
`CID22/CID22-512/training/3571065.png`, native 512x512, no resize, converted
by `identity_run crop:` and fed to BOTH encoders through `perf_encode raw:`.
sha256(.yuv) = 88142a484affd4b36c36ce56ad4e1782811235958b38078e9aa0704d3ca4a72f
-- the same bytes the `callcount_realimg_2026-09-04` record used.

  512x512, photo_cid, port/C     p2        p6
  still                        1.634x    2.101x     both ident=Y
  videokey                     (1.571x)  1.559x     p2 ident=N -- EXCLUDED
  inter                        REFUSED   1.575x

  absolute (ms), port -> C:
    still     p2  1937.328 / 1181.969      p6   68.586 / 32.723
    videokey  p2  1138.877 /  725.939 (N)  p6  173.814 / 111.792
    inter     p2      -    /     -         p6  186.015 / 118.002

One size means NO intercept/slope fit is possible on this arm; only per-cell
ratios are reported, and none is extrapolated to another size.

Reading it against the gradient arm at the same 512x512: still p6 photo 2.101x
against the gradient p8 still 2.208x, and the p2 photo cell is 1.634x -- the
real-content gap is SMALLER than the synthetic one at every comparable cell, the
same direction the earlier record found (2.66x gradient vs 1.80x photo at p2).
The two video arms flip the usual ordering on real content: photo p6 videokey
(1.559x) and inter (1.575x) are BETTER than photo p6 still (2.101x), where on
gradient the video arms are worse than still.

TWO CELLS THAT ARE NOT RESULTS, and why:

  * `photo_cid 512 p2 VIDEOKEY is ident=N`. The port's video-mode KEY frame at
    preset 2 on this image is not byte-identical to C's, so its 1.571x compares
    different work and is excluded from every conclusion above. The same image
    at p2 in STILL mode IS identical, and p6 videokey IS identical, so this is
    specific to (video-mode key frame, preset 2, real content). Reported here
    as a byte finding for whoever owns that axis; not investigated in this
    chunk.

  * `photo_cid 512 p2 INTER produces no timing at all` -- 25 of 25 rounds ERR.
    It is a clean REFUSAL, not a crash. Reproduced directly:

      perf_encode: REFUSED by the encoder at frame 1: unsupported config:
      global motion is not implemented: C svt_aom_derive_gm_level
      (enc_mode_config.c:194) gives an inter frame at preset <= 4 a non-zero
      gm_level, so C searches a model and global_motion_params() codes its type
      and parameters, while this port writes seven is_global = 0 bits and an
      IDENTITY model -- use preset >= 5 for inter frames  [C: accepts]

    exit 3. So there is no p2 inter number to have, on any content, until
    global motion lands. "Not measured" is the honest entry.

=================== HOW TO RE-RUN IT =======================================

  jj workspace add --name pos1 ../zenav1-svt--pos1 -r main@origin
  cd ../zenav1-svt--pos1
  ln -sfn <primary>/reference/svt-av1 reference/svt-av1   # do NOT rebuild the C oracle
  ln -sfn <primary>/Bin Bin ; ln -sfn <primary>/cbuild-static cbuild-static
  export SVT_CREF_LIB_DIR=<primary>/Bin/Release SVT_CREF_SKIP_HDR=1
  export PATH=/opt/homebrew/bin:$PATH        # gawk + nproc the harness needs
  cd rust

  # the real-content .yuv (regenerates byte-for-byte; verify the sha256 above)
  nice -n 19 env -u RUSTFLAGS cargo build --release -p zenav1-svt \
      --example perf_encode --example identity_run -j 8
  ./target/release/examples/identity_run \
      crop:<corpus>/CID22/CID22-512/training/3571065.png 512 512 40 8 \
      ~/tmp/pos1yuv/photo_cid

  # the three gradient arms (the arm9-joining grid)
  G='PERF_SIZES="64 128 256 512" PERF_PRESETS=8 PERF_CONTENT=gradient
     PERF_QP=40 PERF_ROUNDS=25 PERF_WARMUP=1'
  env $G                                        tools/perf_gate.sh <d>-still
  env $G PERF_VIDEO=1                           tools/perf_gate.sh <d>-videokey
  env $G PERF_VIDEO=1 PERF_FRAMES=2 PERF_SHIFT=3 tools/perf_gate.sh <d>-inter

  # the three photo arms (same three configs, one size, presets 2 and 6)
  P='PERF_SIZES=512 PERF_PRESETS="2 6" PERF_QP=40 PERF_ROUNDS=25 PERF_WARMUP=1
     PERF_CONTENT=raw:$HOME/tmp/pos1yuv/photo_cid.yuv'
  env $P                                        tools/perf_gate.sh <d>-photo-still
  env $P PERF_VIDEO=1                           tools/perf_gate.sh <d>-photo-videokey
  env $P PERF_VIDEO=1 PERF_FRAMES=2 PERF_SHIFT=3 tools/perf_gate.sh <d>-photo-inter

  Take a `ps` snapshot before and after each arm and every 15 s inside it, and
  DISCARD any arm whose snapshots show a process that is not the harness's own.

=================== NOT ESTABLISHED ========================================
  * ATTRIBUTION. Nothing here says which of the ~ten landings bought what. A
    position run cannot do that; only a paired A/B of two trees in one session
    can, and none was run in this chunk.
  * WHY the C oracle measured 4-10 % faster this session on an unchanged
    binary. Not investigated.
  * Any preset but 8 on gradient, any size beyond 512, any content but gradient
    and photo_cid, and the whole p2 inter cell (refused).
  * A cross-host comparison. Everything here is the mac; the r7900x was
    reachable and quiet but was NOT used, because arm9 is a mac record and a
    host swap would break the join.
